## 2️⃣ Using `CustomVectorSource` (android-v13.5.0+)

If you prefer to **avoid any URL handling** and instead add the source completely from code, you can use the `CustomVectorSource`. This is handy when you already have a `LifecycleScope` and want to keep the style JSON minimal.

```kotlin
import org.maplibre.android.maps.Style
import org.maplibre.android.style.sources.CustomVectorSource
import org.maplibre.android.style.sources.CustomVectorTileProvider
import org.maplibre.android.style.sources.TileData
import kotlinx.coroutines.CoroutineScope
import kotlinx.coroutines.Dispatchers
import kotlinx.coroutines.launch

// 1️⃣  Create the provider – it receives z/x/y and must return TileData.Mvt
val vaultProvider = object : CustomVectorTileProvider {
    override suspend fun fetchTile(z: Int, x: Int, y: Int): TileData {
        val bytes = Vault.decryptTile(z, x, y)   // your decryption routine
        return TileData.Mvt(bytes)
    }
}

// 2️⃣  Build the source (you can also set maxZoom/minZoom, attribution, etc.)
val vaultSource = CustomVectorSource(
    id = "vault",
    provider = vaultProvider,
    scope = lifecycleScope,   // any CoroutineScope; usually the Activity/Fragment's
    maxZoom = 14
)

// 3️⃣  When the style is loaded, add the source and the layers you need
mapView.getMapAsync { mapboxMap ->
    mapboxMap.setStyle(Style.Builder().fromUri("asset://my_style.json")) { style ->

        // Add the source (no URL in the JSON)
        style.addSource(vaultSource)

        // Add a layer that points at the source
        val fillLayer = FillLayer("my-fill", "vault")
            .withSourceLayer("my_layer_name")
            .withProperties(
                PropertyFactory.fillColor(Color.parseColor("#ff6600")),
                PropertyFactory.fillOpacity(0.5f)
            )
        style.addLayer(fillLayer)
    }
}
```

**Pros**

* No URL parsing at all – you work directly with `z/x/y`.
* Works even if the style JSON is generated server-side and you cannot inject a custom scheme.

**Cons**

* The source and its layers must be added **in code**, not purely via JSON.
* You lose the ability to reference the source from other style JSON files (e.g., if you have multiple style variants that share the same source).

---

## 3️⃣ Intercept via an OkHttp interceptor (android-v11.0.0+)

If your app already configures an `OkHttpClient` for other networking (e.g., authentication, logging) you can replace the client that MapLibre’s default `HttpRequestImpl` uses.

```kotlin
import okhttp3.OkHttpClient
import okhttp3.Protocol
import okhttp3.Response
import okhttp3.ResponseBody.Companion.toResponseBody
import org.maplibre.android.module.http.HttpRequestUtil

val client = OkHttpClient.Builder()
    .addInterceptor { chain ->
        val request = chain.request()
        // Only intercept our custom scheme – everything else goes to the network
        if (request.url.scheme != "vault") {
            return@addInterceptor chain.proceed(request)
        }

        // Parse z/x/y from the path: vault://tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}
        val pathSegments = request.url.pathSegments
        val z = pathSegments[0].toInt()
        val x = pathSegments[1].toInt()
        val y = pathSegments[2].toInt()

        val tileBytes = Vault.decryptTile(z, x, y)

        // Build a synthetic HTTP 200 response that OkHttp will hand back to MapLibre
        Response.Builder()
            .request(request)
            .protocol(Protocol.HTTP_1_1)
            .code(200)
            .message("OK")
            .body(tileBytes.toResponseBody(null))   // null = unknown MIME type (MVT)
            .build()
    }
    .build()

// Tell MapLibre to use this client for *all* its HTTP work
HttpRequestUtil.setOkHttpClient(client)
```

> **Note:** The built-in `HttpRequestImpl` will *first* try to parse the URL with `HttpUrl.parse`. If the scheme is unknown (`vault://`) it will reject the URL **before** the interceptor sees it. Therefore you must keep the scheme **exactly** as `vault://` (lower-case) and **not** use any characters that OkHttp’s parser rejects (e.g., spaces). The interceptor approach works because the request reaches OkHttp **after** MapLibre has handed the URL to the default `HttpRequestImpl`, which simply forwards the raw string to OkHttp.

