## 1️⃣ Intercept `vault://` with a custom `HttpRequest` (the closest analogue to `addProtocol`)

### How it works
* MapLibre creates a single `HttpRequest` object that is used for **every** network-type resource (style, sprites, glyphs, vector tiles, raster tiles, …).  
* By installing a `ModuleProvider` you can replace that request with your own implementation.  
* Inside `executeRequest` you decide whether to let the stock request handle the URL or to answer it yourself.

### Boiler-plate you need
```kotlin
import org.maplibre.android.MapLibre
import org.maplibre.android.ModuleProvider
import org.maplibre.android.http.HttpRequest
import org.maplibre.android.http.HttpResponder

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1️⃣  Your own decryptor – replace with your real code
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
object Vault {
    /** Return the raw MVT bytes for the given vault URL */
    fun decryptTile(url: String): ByteArray {
        // Example: vault://tiles/12/3456/7890  →  decrypt and return the MVT bytes
        // Throw if the tile cannot be produced – the responder will turn it into an error.
        TODO("Your decryption logic here")
    }
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2️⃣  Wrapper that delegates to the stock request when the URL is NOT vault://
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
class VaultHttpRequest(private val stock: HttpRequest) : HttpRequest {

    override fun executeRequest(
        responder: HttpResponder,
        nativePtr: Long,
        resourceUrl: String,
        dataRange: String?,   // “bytes=0-” etc. – ignore for MVT
        etag: String?,
        modified: String?,
        offlineUsage: Boolean
    ) {
        // ------------------------------------------------------------------
        // If it isn’t a vault URL, just forward to the default implementation
        // ------------------------------------------------------------------
        if (!resourceUrl.startsWith("vault://")) {
            stock.executeRequest(
                responder,
                nativePtr,
                resourceUrl,
                dataRange,
                etag,
                modified,
                offlineUsage
            )
            return
        }

        // ------------------------------------------------------------------
        // 3️⃣  Handle the vault URL yourself
        // ------------------------------------------------------------------
        try {
            val bytes = Vault.decryptTile(resourceUrl)   // ← your decryption routine
            // The responder expects the same arguments as a normal HTTP response.
            // Most of them can be null for a simple 200 OK.
            responder.onResponse(
                200,               // HTTP status
                null,              // content-type (MVT is binary, MapLibre ignores it)
                null,              // cache-control
                null,              // expires
                null,              // eTag
                null,              // last-modified
                null,              // retry-after
                bytes               // body
            )
        } catch (e: Exception) {
            // --------------------------------------------------------------
            // 4️⃣  Report a failure – choose the most appropriate type
            // --------------------------------------------------------------
            responder.handleFailure(
                HttpRequest.CONNECTION_ERROR,   // or TEMPORARY_ERROR / PERMANENT_ERROR
                e.localizedMessage ?: "Vault decryption failed"
            )
        }
    }

    override fun cancelRequest() = stock.cancelRequest()
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 5️⃣  Install the provider **before** any MapView is created
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
fun installVaultModuleProvider() {
    // Grab the default provider that ships with the SDK
    val stockProvider = MapLibre.getModuleProvider()

    // Wrap it so that only vault URLs are intercepted
    MapLibre.setModuleProvider(object : ModuleProvider by stockProvider {
        override fun createHttpRequest(): HttpRequest = VaultHttpRequest(stockProvider.createHttpRequest())
    })
}
```

**Where to call it**

```kotlin
class MyApplication : Application() {
    override fun onCreate() {
        super.onCreate()
        // Must be done before the first MapView is inflated
        installVaultModuleProvider()
    }
}
```

### What the style looks like
```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "sources": {
    "myVault": {
      "type": "vector",
      "url": "vault://tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}"
    }
  },
  "layers": [
    {
      "id": "my-data",
      "type": "fill",
      "source": "myVault",
      "source-layer": "my_layer_name",
      "paint": { "fill-color": "#ff6600", "fill-opacity": 0.5 }
    }
  ]
}
```

*No other code changes are required.*  
If the style also references sprites, glyphs, or other resources with `vault://`, they will be served by the same interceptor automatically.

---

## 2️⃣ Build the source in code with `CustomVectorSource` (no URL needed)

If you prefer **not to touch the style JSON at all**, you can create a vector source entirely from Kotlin and feed it tiles via a `CustomVectorTileProvider`. This method was added in **android-v13.5.0**.

### Kotlin example

```kotlin
import androidx.lifecycle.lifecycleScope
import org.maplibre.android.maps.Style
import org.maplibre.android.style.layers.FillLayer
import org.maplibre.android.style.sources.CustomVectorSource
import org.maplibre.android.style.sources.CustomVectorTileProvider
import org.maplibre.android.style.sources.TileData

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1️⃣  Provider that returns raw MVT bytes for a given z/x/y
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
class VaultTileProvider : CustomVectorTileProvider {
    override suspend fun fetchTile(z: Int, x: Int, y: Int): TileData {
        // Build the vault URL just for logging / debugging – you can skip it.
        val vaultUrl = "vault://tiles/$z/$x/$y"
        val bytes = Vault.decryptTile(vaultUrl)   // reuse the same decryptor as above
        return TileData.Mvt(bytes)               // tell MapLibre this is an MVT tile
    }
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2️⃣  Add the source & a layer after the style is loaded
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
mapView.getMapAsync { mapboxMap ->
    mapboxMap.setStyle(Style.Builder().fromUri("asset://my_style.json")) { style ->

        // Create the custom source
        val vaultSource = CustomVectorSource(
            id = "vault-source",
            provider = VaultTileProvider(),
            scope = lifecycleScope,   // coroutine scope for async fetches
            maxZoom = 14              // optional – limit zoom if you like
        )
        style.addSource(vaultSource)

        // Add a layer that uses the source
        val fill = FillLayer("vault-fill", "vault-source")
            .withSourceLayer("my_layer_name")
            .withProperties(
                PropertyFactory.fillColor("#ff6600"),
                PropertyFactory.fillOpacity(0.5f)
            )
        style.addLayer(fill)
    }
}
```

**Pros**

* No need to modify the style JSON (the source is added programmatically).  
* You have full control over the tile-fetching coroutine (you can add caching, rate-limiting, etc.).

**Cons**

* The source and its layers are **not** described in the JSON, so any external style editor won’t see them.  
* Requires Android v13.5.0+.

---

## 3️⃣ Use an OkHttp interceptor (if you already use OkHttp)

MapLibre’s default `HttpRequest` implementation delegates to **OkHttp** under the hood. By swapping the client you can intercept any `https://` request and return the decrypted bytes. This is handy when you want to keep the style JSON unchanged but you cannot (or do not want to) use the `ModuleProvider` hook.

### Set a custom OkHttp client

```kotlin
import okhttp3.OkHttpClient
import okhttp3.Protocol
import okhttp3.Response
import okhttp3.ResponseBody.Companion.toResponseBody
import org.maplibre.android.module.http.HttpRequestUtil

val client = OkHttpClient.Builder()
    .addInterceptor { chain ->
        val request = chain.request()
        // Decide which URLs you want to handle – here we look for a query param,
        // but you could also check the host/scheme/path.
        if (request.url.host != "vault.example.com") {
            // Not a vault request → let the network handle it
            return@addInterceptor chain.proceed(request)
        }

        // Build the vault URL that your decryptor expects.
        // Example: https://vault.example.com/tiles/12/3456/7890
        val vaultUrl = "vault://${request.url.encodedPath}"
        val bytes = Vault.decryptTile(vaultUrl)

        // Build a synthetic OkHttp response that MapLibre will treat as a normal HTTP 200.
        Response.Builder()
            .request(request)
            .protocol(Protocol.HTTP_1_1)
            .code(200)
            .message("OK")
            .body(bytes.toResponseBody())   // content-type can be omitted
            .build()
    }
    .build()

// Tell MapLibre to use this client for *all* its HTTP work
HttpRequestUtil.setOkHttpClient(client)
```

**Important notes**

* The interceptor **must be installed before the first `MapView`** is created (otherwise the default client is already cached).  
* The URL you intercept can be any scheme you like, but the default `HttpRequest` **rejects** URLs it cannot parse (e.g. `vault://`). Therefore you usually rewrite the style to use `https://vault.example.com/…` (or add a query param) and let the interceptor catch those.  
* If you need to keep the exact `vault://` string in the style, you must use the `ModuleProvider` approach – the OkHttp path cannot see `vault://` because the request never reaches OkHttp.

---

## 🎯 Which method should you pick?

| Situation | Best fit |
|-----------|----------|
| **You already have a style JSON that uses `vault://…` and you want zero-change on the style side** | **Method 1 – custom `HttpRequest` via `ModuleProvider`** (closest to `addProtocol`). |
| **You are comfortable creating the source in code and don’t need the source to appear in the JSON** | **Method 2 – `CustomVectorSource`** (simpler, no URL parsing). |
| **Your app already uses OkHttp for other networking and you prefer a single interceptor** | **Method 3 – OkHttp interceptor** (but you’ll have to change the URLs to something OkHttp can parse, e.g. `https://vault.example.com/...`). |

All three approaches are fully supported in the **current Android release (android-v13.7.1)** and will continue to work in future releases because they are part of the public SDK.

---

## 📚 Quick reference cheat-sheet

| API / Class | What it does | Since |
|-------------|--------------|-------|
| `MapLibre.setModuleProvider(ModuleProvider)` | Replaces the global request factory. | 11.0.0 |
| `ModuleProvider.createHttpRequest()` | Returns the `HttpRequest` used for every network resource. | 11.0.0 |
| `HttpRequest.executeRequest(...)` | Called for each tile / sprite / glyph request. | 11.0.0 |
| `HttpResponder.onResponse(status, …, body)` | Feed back raw bytes (MVT, PNG, …). | 11.0.0 |
| `CustomVectorSource` + `CustomVectorTileProvider` | Programmatic vector source that asks you for raw MVT bytes. | 13.5.0 |
| `HttpRequestUtil.setOkHttpClient(OkHttpClient)` | Swap the underlying OkHttp client (enables interceptors). | 11.0.0 |

